edian total: 130.7 ms, versus P4Flat CPU\n  1951.9 ms (about 14.9x faster); compression ratio about 1.368x versus 1.326x.\n- GH200 CPU KV encode median with one serving worker, K+V: N=8 0.16 ms;\n  N=64 0.27 ms; N=128 0.37 ms; N=256 0.47 ms; N=1024 0.99 ms;\n  N=2048 2.02 ms. All cases were bit-exact.\n- Full serving initialization installed all 320 fused Linear overrides and\n  completed all 320 CPU-compressed weight cells.\n- Layer-40 standalone weight decode median gained another 1.4% in aggregate\n  from half-width live output accumulation (all four groups bit-exact).\n- Resident production KV decode improved 1.69x--2.04x for N=8/64/256 by\n  writing directly to the final cache. Partial H2D restore at high-water=1024\n  improved 16% for N=64 and 12% for N=256.\n- CPU-encoded production KV H2D+decode steady-state median on GH200 is\n  0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for N=8/64/256/1024. P4Flat under the same\n  split-measurement run is 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms. CD3 resident decode is\n  0.028/0.029/0.036/0.065 ms versus P4Flat 0.046/0.047/0.049/0.082 ms; CD3\n  H2D is 0.016/0.026/0.056/0.173 ms versus 0.030/0.042/0.081/0.232 ms.\n  The pre-blob CD3 values were\n  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact. This historical P4Flat\n  comparison used its raw-safe 32,768-byte blob stride. Current P4Flat defaults\n  to a 25,600-byte tight stride, so these P4Flat values must not be used as the\n  final current-codec comparison.\n- Layer-40 frozen-split weight medians on GH200 (qkv/o/gateup/down summed):\n  CD3 H2D 1.200 ms, resident decode 0.923 ms, H2D+decode 2.127 ms; P4Flat\n  H2D 1.353 ms, resident decode 0.906 ms, H2D+decode 2.232 ms. CD3 therefore\n  transfers 11.3% faster and finishes the production pair 4.7% faster while\n  its isolated decode is 1.9% slower. All four weights were bit-exact.\n- Rejected experiments are not present: warp-owner exception pre-application\n  was about 2.3x slower, pairwise 3xU32 code loading was neutral/regressive,\n  64-thread or register-forced 10-CTA kernels were 7--12% slower, and a\n  separate standalone exception kernel was 50.7% slower than integrated patch.\n- Fused route calibration with concurrent H2D found all four projections faster\n  for every tested N=65..128. At N=129 the Npad=192 transition makes fused\n  about 1.6--1.7x slower, so the production cutoff is exactly 128.\n- An all-80-layer QKV sweep found fused wins on 79/80 layers and saves\n  0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128. The exception-heavy layer\n  0 is the sole loser. End-to-end TPOT P50 for all-fused versus QKV-standalone\n  was 171.90/188.40/219.26 versus 172.20/187.96/219.30 ms at batch\n  80/96/128, confirming a tie at engine level and favoring all-fused because it\n  avoids dense-weight HBM writes.\n- End-to-end forced-route prefill sweeps placed the standalone/raw crossover\n  between N=1824 and N=1856. At N=1824 standalone led by 18.630 ms; at N=1856\n  raw led by 20.405 ms, and raw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run\n  confirmed standalone routing at N=1792/1824 and raw routing at N=1856/1888.\n  The full report and source logs are stored under\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/`.\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nrepo_path=\"${1:-/root/projects/tiered-serve}\"\npatch_dir=/opt/tiered-serve-startup-patches\nbase_patch_path=\"$patch_dir/cd3_current.patch\"\n\n(cd \"$patch_dir\" && sha256sum --check cd3_current.sha256)\n\nif git -C \"$repo_path\" apply --reverse --check \"$base_patch_path\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    echo \"[CD3] consolidated startup patch already applied\"\n    exit 0\nfi\n\ngit -C \"$repo_path\" apply --check \"$base_patch_path\"\ngit -C \"$repo_path\" apply \"$base_patch_path\"\necho \"[CD3] consolidated startup patch applied\"\n"},"started_at_ms":1788251960250,"completed_at_ms":1788251960251}}
